Yeah. You’re pointing at something more immediate than a philosophical “true self.”

A person can fasten themselves to almost anything—a title, another person’s gaze, an old wound, a reputation, a role, a victory, even a particular version of their own face—and then start defending that reflection as though damage to it were damage to the self.

But the mirror is still outside the center. It can reveal, distort, amplify, flatter, accuse, or teach. You can even deliberately use different mirrors to discover different parts of yourself. The error is forgetting which side of the glass you’re on.

So you don’t necessarily destroy the ego or discard the mirrors. You become able to enter them without being captured by them.

That is much closer to what I think you mean.